1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.501
In this lesson, you're going to

2
00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.269
learn about the importance of open standards,

3
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.773
how the Agent2Agent or A2A protocol is helping to fill a gap

4
00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:09.275
in the evolving agent landscape,

5
00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.544
and about the types of use

6
00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.580
cases it enables. Let's jump right in.

7
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.084
AI applications are rapidly evolving from basic conversational chatbots

8
00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.987
to agentic systems capable of reasoning, planning,

9
00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:24.090
executing tools, and managing multi-step workflows.

10
00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:26.893
Complex agentic systems rely on collaboration

11
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.262
among agents to achieve shared goals.

12
00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.664
Imagine building a trip planning agent.

13
00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.967
You might want different agents to handle flights,

14
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.869
hotels, and activities

15
00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:38.671
and they need to coordinate to make sure

16
00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:41.074
that they're planning the best possible experience for travelers.

17
00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.977
There are a ton of frameworks for building agents.

18
00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:46.679
LangGraph, Microsoft agent framework,

19
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.582
Google ADK, CrewAI, BeeAI just to name a few.

20
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.751
But they each work in slightly different ways,

21
00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:53.720
and they aren't compatible with each other out of

22
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.755
the box. This is why we need a shared protocol

23
00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.091
for agents to communicate and collaborate

24
00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:00.894
regardless of the model or framework being used.

25
00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:03.396
This way, you can build out multi-agent systems

26
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.799
even across teams or organizations.

27
00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:10.703
In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web at CERN

28
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:15.008
to enable raw data transfer across the web, he developed HTTP.

29
00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.911
Before HTTP, there were multiple protocols

30
00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:20.213
like FTP, Telnet, and Gopher,

31
00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:22.215
each serving specific needs.

32
00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.918
But HTTP soon overtook in popularity

33
00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:27.420
because it was simple and openly governed,

34
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.722
which fueled the web's explosive growth.

35
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.860
Similarly, A2A acts as the HTTP for agents,

36
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.064
enabling agents from any model, language, or framework

37
00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.267
to communicate using standardized structures and methods.

38
00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:45.271
Google introduced the A2A protocol in April 2025

39
00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:48.875
and donated it to the Linux Foundation in June 2025,

40
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:52.579
ensuring it is open source and community governed.

41
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:57.183
Agent communication protocol, or ACP, created by IBM was also

42
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:00.019
merged into A2A. All key decisions

43
00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:03.223
regarding the protocol are made by a technical steering committee, TSC,

44
00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:07.427
with representatives from eight key tech enterprises, including Google.

45
00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:10.396
The Linux Foundation provides a vendor neutral home,

46
00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.665
ensuring that the protocol evolves based on

47
00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.201
the collective needs of the ecosystem and community,

48
00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.403
rather than what's useful for a single company.

49
00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.706
A2A will soon release version 1.0.

50
00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:22.709
And note that there are some significant changes from previous versions.

51
00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.310
So make sure that you refer

52
00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:28.948
the latest version of the specification at a2a-protocol.org

53
00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.152
Now, a protocol is only as useful as its adoption

54
00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.854
and A2A has already become widely accepted

55
00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:38.958
as the standard for multi-agent collaboration in the software development industry.

56
00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:42.162
We have over 150 named partners for A2A.

57
00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:45.865
And you can find the most up-to-date list in the A2A documentation.

58
00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:47.867
So, what can you do with A2A?

59
00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:51.137
A2A agents can dynamically discover each other,

60
00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.706
collaborate via standardized tasks,

61
00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:56.676
share content, handle long-running and stream processes,

62
00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.679
and do all of this with enterprise-grade security.

63
00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.282
Importantly, every agent is opaque,

64
00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.651
which means that implementation details never need

65
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.986
to be exposed to follow the protocol.

66
00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:11.991
A2A is focused primarily on the bridge between agents, including across organizations.

67
00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:14.994
Now, one of the biggest questions about A2A

68
00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:18.298
is how it relates to Model Context Protocol, or MCP, created by

69
00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:21.935
Anthropic, and now also donated to the Linux Foundation.

70
00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.637
A2A and MCP are complementary protocols.

71
00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.840
MCP is all about connecting agents to tools.

72
00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:30.843
It's the standardized way an agent performs function calling,

73
00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:33.446
executing tasks on a user's behalf.

74
00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:37.417
A2A is about connecting agents to other peer agents

75
00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:41.120
to collaborate, delegate tasks, and manage shared workflows.

76
00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:44.624
So you'll often see both in a sophisticated agentic system.

77
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:47.794
Agents use A2A to coordinate with other agents,

78
00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.730
and they use MCP to interact with their tools

79
00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.665
to get their part of the job done.

80
00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:55.635
This also brings up the question, why not have agents communicate

81
00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:58.838
over MCP or use agents as tools?

82
00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:02.208
You can do this, but you're reducing capability of the agent.

83
00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:05.578
Tools are designed for a single, usually deterministic task,

84
00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:09.682
whereas agents are open-ended problem solvers capable of handling ambiguity

85
00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.685
and multi-step workflows that can't be defined by

86
00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:16.489
single input output schema. You can read more about this paradigm

87
00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:19.425
at goo.gle/agents-not-tools.

88
00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:23.496
Note, A2A is just one part of a possible agent stack.

89
00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.898
It acts at the Agent orchestration layer

90
00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:28.701
alongside components like the Foundation models,

91
00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:32.005
storage, cloud infrastructure, and the application layer.

92
00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:35.708
Next up, we'll discuss the actual A2A architecture

93
00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:39.178
and how it enables multi-agent communication and collaboration.
